data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":4227,"item_id":"fc_0bb18d10865444c7016ae21a108f508191a2a38702eb9729f5","output_index":2,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":2,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/steps\",\"value\":[{\"id\":\"T001\",\"statement\":\"ScaleLaws\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S001\"],\"explanation\":\"Apply native hausdorffMeasure_apply; at positive exponents empty summands vanish. Compare positive finite real scales with extended scales, including infinity. Prove scale antitonicity and eventual unboundedness directly from the supremum. The structure is only a bundle of propositions to prove, not an assumed fact.\"},{\"id\":\"T002\",\"statement\":\"CriticalLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S002\"],\"explanation\":\"Use native critical-exponent dimension theorems with the nonnegative real corresponding to s>0; convert via T001. Native dimH already takes values in ENNReal.\"},{\"id\":\"T003\",\"statement\":\"InvarianceLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\",\"T002\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S003\"],\"explanation\":\"Cover inclusion and the image/preimage cover argument for subtype isometries give measure and dimension invariance; direct library theorems may implement these arguments. General metric formulations specialize to every Euclidean space used below.\"},{\"id\":\"T004\",\"statement\":\"ScaleLaws ∧ CriticalLaws ∧ InvarianceLaws ∧ PowerLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\",\"T002\",\"T003\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S001\",\"S002\",\"S003\",\"S004\"],\"explanation\":\"Package N001 in explicit proposition structures. Prove the two rpow limits and geometric ENNReal series with native APIs or the supplied finite-sum argument. Native tsum on nonnegative terms is the supremum of finite subsums (hence initial partial sums for ℕ); no signed-series convergence is asserted.\"},{\"id\":\"T005\",\"statement\":\"(∃ R : ℝ, 1 ≤ R ∧ ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ j : Fin n, |x j| ≤ R) ∧ Bornology.IsBounded E ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E < ⊤)\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\"],\"explanation\":\"Compactness gives a containing box and a finite subcover of balls of radius δ/2. Pad each finite cover with empty sets to bound the content by a finite cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T006\",\"statement\":\"(0 ≤ dimH E ∧ dimH E ≤ (n : ℝ≥0∞)) ∧ (n = 0 → E = Set.univ ∧ dimH E = 0)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S006\"],\"explanation\":\"For n>0 subdivide the containing box into ceil(2R/h)^n cubes, bound diameters by sqrt(n)h and a-cost by a constant times h^(a-n), then send h to zero. For n=0 use the unique empty tuple and a singleton cover. This retains the supplied finite-dimensional covering argument.\"},{\"id\":\"T007\",\"statement\":\"Function.Bijective (fun p : V n × ℝ => join p.1 p.2) ∧ (∀ (x : V n) (t : ℝ), base (join x t) = x ∧ height (join x t) = t) ∧ (∀ z : V (n+1), join (base z) (height z) = z) ∧ (∀ (x y : V n) (t u : ℝ), dist (join x t) (join y u) ^ 2 = dist x y ^ 2 + dist t u ^ 2)\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S007\"],\"explanation\":\"Fin.lastCases concatenation and coordinate restriction are inverse, and the finite sum of squared coordinate differences splits into its first n coordinates and last coordinate. This is the checked Euclidean product representation bridge; the domain pair type is used only as a set of coordinates, not with its native max metric.\"},{\"id\":\"T008\",\"statement\":\"(0 ≤ dimH E ∧ dimH E ≤ (n : ℝ≥0∞)) ∧ dimH E ≠ ⊤ ∧ Bornology.IsBounded E ∧ (∃ R : ℝ, 1 ≤ R ∧ ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ j : Fin n, |x j| ≤ R) ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E < ⊤) ∧ (n = 0 → E = Set.univ ∧ dimH E = 0) ∧ Function.Bijective (fun p : V n × ℝ => join p.1 p.2) ∧ (∀ (x : V n) (t : ℝ), base (join x t) = x ∧ height (join x t) = t) ∧ (∀ z : V (n+1), join (base z) (height z) = z) ∧ (∀ (x y : V n) (t u : ℝ), dist (join x t) (join y u) ^ 2 = dist x y ^ 2 + dist t u ^ 2)\",\"inputs\":[\"T005\",\"T006\",\"T007\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\",\"S006\",\"S007\"],\"explanation\":\"Assemble every N002 clause. The dimension bound by the finite natural n gives finiteness; the inverse and squared-distance identities express exactly the bijective Euclidean concatenation isometry without installing a different product metric.\"},{\"id\":\"T009\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q r ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < r → r ≤ 1 → 0 < ε → ∃ W : ℕ → Set (V n), scaleCover r E W ∧ cost q W < ENNReal.ofReal (ε/2)\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S008\"],\"explanation\":\"q>D forces q>0, and the native critical-exponent statement gives zero Hausdorff measure and zero content. Extract an admissible sequence of cost below ε/2 from the infimum.\"},{\"id\":\"T010\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q r ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < r → r ≤ 1 → 0 < ε → ∃ K : Set ℕ, ∃ U : K → Set (V n), E ⊆ (⋃ i, U i) ∧ (∀ i, (U i).Nonempty ∧ 0 < Metric.ediam (U i) ∧ Metric.ediam (U i) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal r) ∧ cost q U < ENNReal.ofReal ε\",\"inputs\":[\"T009\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S009\"],\"explanation\":\"N003: remove empty members using a subtype of ℕ. Replace zero-diameter singletons by small closed balls as in S009. In n≥1 opposite first-coordinate points certify the prescribed positive diameter. Bound extra cost by the geometric error budget. Subtypes of ℕ capture arbitrary countable index sets up to enumeration.\"},{\"id\":\"T011\",\"statement\":\"n = 0 → dimH (cylinder E) ≤ dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S010\"],\"explanation\":\"For the unique base point partition the interval into m pieces; joined pieces have diameters 1/m by the squared-distance identity. For a>1 the total cost tends to zero, giving the zero-ambient-dimensional upper bound.\"},{\"id\":\"T012\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q δ ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → ∃ W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1)), scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W ∧ cost (q+1) W < ENNReal.ofReal (2 * (Real.sqrt 2) ^ (q+1) * ε)\",\"inputs\":[\"T010\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S011\"],\"explanation\":\"Take r=min(1,δ/sqrt 2), and the N003 cover. For each member of diameter r_i, use ceil(1/r_i) subintervals and their joined products. Enumerate the countable sigma family with empty padding. The squared-distance identity bounds product diameters by sqrt(2)r_i; ceil(1/r_i)≤2/r_i yields the stated cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T013\",\"statement\":\"dimH (cylinder E) ≤ dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T011\",\"T012\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S012\"],\"explanation\":\"N004: let ε decrease to zero at fixed q>D and δ>0, then take the supremum over scales and the dimension bound at exponent q+1. Finiteness of D and order density allow q to decrease to D. Combine n=0 and n≥1.\"},{\"id\":\"T014\",\"statement\":\"MeasurableSet unitI ∧ (volume unitI : ℝ≥0∞) = 1\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S013\"],\"explanation\":\"Use Borel measurability of Icc and Real.volume_Icc.\"},{\"id\":\"T015\",\"statement\":\"(MeasurableSet unitI ∧ (volume unitI : ℝ≥0∞) = 1) ∧ (∀ (B : Set ℝ) (ρ η : ℝ), B.Nonempty → 0 ≤ ρ → Metric.ediam B ≤ ENNReal.ofReal ρ → 0 < η → Bornology.IsBounded B ∧ BddBelow B ∧ BddAbove B ∧ (∀ b ∈ B, sInf B ≤ b ∧ b ≤ sSup B) ∧ sSup B - sInf B ≤ ρ ∧ (let J := Set.Ioo (sInf B - η/2) (sSup B + η/2); B ⊆ J ∧ MeasurableSet J ∧ 0 < sSup B - sInf B + η ∧ sSup B - sInf B + η ≤ ρ + η ∧ (volume J : ℝ≥0∞) = ENNReal.ofReal (sSup B - sInf B + η) ∧ (volume (J ∩ unitI) : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (ρ + η) ∧ MeasurableSet (J ∩ unitI)))\",\"inputs\":[\"T014\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S014\"],\"explanation\":\"N005: finite ediam and nonemptiness imply boundedness. Real infimum/supremum inequalities give sup-inf≤ρ. The displayed open interval encloses B and has length at most ρ+η; apply volume_Ioo and measure monotonicity. No measurability of B is used.\"},{\"id\":\"T016\",\"statement\":\"LipschitzWith 1 (base (n := n)) ∧ LipschitzWith 1 (height (n := n)) ∧ (∀ (δ : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < δ → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → ∀ i, Metric.ediam (W i) = ENNReal.ofReal (Metric.diam (W i)) ∧ 0 ≤ Metric.diam (W i) ∧ Metric.diam (W i) ≤ δ ∧ Metric.ediam (base '' W i) ≤ Metric.ediam (W i) ∧ Metric.ediam (height '' W i) ≤ Metric.ediam (W i))\",\"inputs\":[\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S015\"],\"explanation\":\"The coordinate splitting of squared Euclidean distances gives 1-Lipschitz projections. Their image diameter bounds follow. At finite scale every ediam is finite, so Metric.diam and ENNReal.ofReal are exact inverse descriptions; this is the explicit real/extended-diameter conversion used in the error budget.\"},{\"id\":\"T017\",\"statement\":\"∀ (s δ ε : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < s → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → ∃ J : ℕ → Set ℝ, (∀ i, MeasurableSet (J i) ∧ height '' W i ⊆ J i ∧ (volume (J i ∩ unitI) : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (Metric.diam (W i) + padding ε s W i)) ∧ (∀ t ∈ unitI, content s δ E ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i) (fun _ : ℝ => (Metric.ediam (W i)) ^ s) t)\",\"inputs\":[\"T015\",\"T016\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S016\"],\"explanation\":\"Use η_i=ε2^(-i-1)/(1+ρ_i^s)>0, explicit enclosures for nonempty W_i and J_i=∅ otherwise. For every t∈I, choose projected sets when t∈J_i and empty sets otherwise. This is a scale-δ cover of E and its content cost is bounded by the indicator series. Arbitrary covering sets and their projections need not be measurable.\"},{\"id\":\"T018\",\"statement\":\"∀ (c : ℝ≥0∞) (w : ℕ → ℝ≥0∞) (J : ℕ → Set ℝ), (∀ i, MeasurableSet (J i)) → (∀ t ∈ unitI, c ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i) (fun _ : ℝ => w i) t) → ((∀ i, Measurable (Set.indicator (J i ∩ unitI) (fun _ : ℝ => w i))) ∧ (∀ t : ℝ, Set.indicator unitI (fun _ : ℝ => c) t ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i ∩ unitI) (fun _ : ℝ => w i) t) ∧ c ≤ ∑' i, w i * volume (J i ∩ unitI))\",\"inputs\":[\"T014\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S017\",\"S018\"],\"explanation\":\"Prove measurability of the Borel indicators and the pointwise inequality on all ℝ by cases t∈I. Integrate using lintegral_mono, lintegral_tsum and lintegral_indicator_const, and volume(I)=1. This nonnegative Tonelli statement allows infinite c and infinite series and requires no finite integrability.\"},{\"id\":\"T019\",\"statement\":\"∀ (s δ ε : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < s → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → content s δ E ≤ cost (s+1) W + ENNReal.ofReal ε\",\"inputs\":[\"T017\",\"T018\",\"T016\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S018\"],\"explanation\":\"Insert the enclosures into T018. With finite ρ_i convert the real algebra η_iρ_i^s≤ε2^(-i-1) back to ENNReal, and use ρ_i^sρ_i=ρ_i^(s+1). Sum using nonnegative tsum comparisons and the geometric sum, valid also for infinite total cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T020\",\"statement\":\"∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E ≤ content (s+1) δ (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T019\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S019\"],\"explanation\":\"N006: for each admissible W remove the arbitrary positive ε using order density (infinite cost is immediate). Infimize over W. Retain exactly the same scale on both sides.\"},{\"id\":\"T021\",\"statement\":\"0 < dimH E → ∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → ENNReal.ofReal s < dimH E → haus (s+1) (cylinder E) = ⊤ ∧ ENNReal.ofReal (s+1) ≤ dimH (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T020\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S020\"],\"explanation\":\"For 0<s<D criticality gives haus s E=∞. Take suprema in the same-scale slicing inequality, then apply criticality at s+1.\"},{\"id\":\"T022\",\"statement\":\"0 < dimH E → dimH E + 1 ≤ dimH (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T021\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S021\"],\"explanation\":\"N007: finite positive D is the supremum of the positive real s<D. Apply order density to the inequalities at s+1.\"},{\"id\":\"T023\",\"statement\":\"(∀ a : ℝ, 1 < a → haus a unitI = 0) ∧ dimH unitI ≤ 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\"],\"explanation\":\"Divide [0,1] into m equal intervals. Their a-cost is m^(1-a), tending to zero, and diameters tend to zero. Apply criticality and order density.\"},{\"id\":\"T024\",\"statement\":\"∀ (δ : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set ℝ), 0 < δ → scaleCover δ unitI W → (1 : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ cost 1 W\",\"inputs\":[\"T015\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S023\"],\"explanation\":\"Enclose each nonempty member by an open interval with error ε2^(-i-1), use volume(I)=1 and countable subadditivity, sum the geometric errors and remove ε. This proves total diameter at least one without assuming the cover measurable.\"},{\"id\":\"T025\",\"statement\":\"(∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → 0 < δ → ENNReal.ofReal (δ ^ (s-1)) ≤ content s δ unitI) ∧ (∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → haus s unitI = ⊤) ∧ (1 : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ dimH unitI\",\"inputs\":[\"T024\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S024\"],\"explanation\":\"At finite scale compare ρ_i^s≥δ^(s-1)ρ_i, including ρ_i=0 separately. Sum and infimize. The negative-power limit gives infinite Hausdorff measure and criticality gives dimension at least one.\"},{\"id\":\"T026\",\"statement\":\"(∀ a : ℝ, 1 < a → haus a unitI = 0) ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → 0 < δ → ENNReal.ofReal (δ ^ (s-1)) ≤ content s δ unitI) ∧ (∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → haus s unitI = ⊤) ∧ dimH unitI = 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T023\",\"T025\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\",\"S023\",\"S024\"],\"explanation\":\"N008: assemble the interval content and measure assertions and the equality of upper and lower dimension bounds.\"},{\"id\":\"T027\",\"statement\":\"dimH (cylinder E) = dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T013\",\"T022\",\"T026\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S025\"],\"explanation\":\"If D>0 apply N007. If D=0 choose x₀∈E, use the squared-distance identity to show t↦join x₀ t is an isometry on I, and its image lies in cylinder E. Isometry invariance, monotonicity and N008 yield dimension at least one. Combine with N004; T008 identifies exactly the stipulated Euclidean target, including n=0.\"}]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/source_outputs\",\"value\":{\"N001\":\"T004\",\"N002\":\"T008\",\"N003\":\"T010\",\"N004\":\"T013\",\"N005\":\"T015\",\"N006\":\"T020\",\"N007\":\"T022\",\"N008\":\"T026\",\"P\":\"T027\"}}]}}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":4228,"output_index":2,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0bb18d10865444c7016ae21a108f508191a2a38702eb9729f5","call_id":"call_a38dd5ada7054eadbca9620701a15693","name":"save_interface_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":2,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/steps\",\"value\":[{\"id\":\"T001\",\"statement\":\"ScaleLaws\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S001\"],\"explanation\":\"Apply native hausdorffMeasure_apply; at positive exponents empty summands vanish. Compare positive finite real scales with extended scales, including infinity. Prove scale antitonicity and eventual unboundedness directly from the supremum. The structure is only a bundle of propositions to prove, not an assumed fact.\"},{\"id\":\"T002\",\"statement\":\"CriticalLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S002\"],\"explanation\":\"Use native critical-exponent dimension theorems with the nonnegative real corresponding to s>0; convert via T001. Native dimH already takes values in ENNReal.\"},{\"id\":\"T003\",\"statement\":\"InvarianceLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\",\"T002\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S003\"],\"explanation\":\"Cover inclusion and the image/preimage cover argument for subtype isometries give measure and dimension invariance; direct library theorems may implement these arguments. General metric formulations specialize to every Euclidean space used below.\"},{\"id\":\"T004\",\"statement\":\"ScaleLaws ∧ CriticalLaws ∧ InvarianceLaws ∧ PowerLaws\",\"inputs\":[\"T001\",\"T002\",\"T003\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S001\",\"S002\",\"S003\",\"S004\"],\"explanation\":\"Package N001 in explicit proposition structures. Prove the two rpow limits and geometric ENNReal series with native APIs or the supplied finite-sum argument. Native tsum on nonnegative terms is the supremum of finite subsums (hence initial partial sums for ℕ); no signed-series convergence is asserted.\"},{\"id\":\"T005\",\"statement\":\"(∃ R : ℝ, 1 ≤ R ∧ ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ j : Fin n, |x j| ≤ R) ∧ Bornology.IsBounded E ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E < ⊤)\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\"],\"explanation\":\"Compactness gives a containing box and a finite subcover of balls of radius δ/2. Pad each finite cover with empty sets to bound the content by a finite cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T006\",\"statement\":\"(0 ≤ dimH E ∧ dimH E ≤ (n : ℝ≥0∞)) ∧ (n = 0 → E = Set.univ ∧ dimH E = 0)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S006\"],\"explanation\":\"For n>0 subdivide the containing box into ceil(2R/h)^n cubes, bound diameters by sqrt(n)h and a-cost by a constant times h^(a-n), then send h to zero. For n=0 use the unique empty tuple and a singleton cover. This retains the supplied finite-dimensional covering argument.\"},{\"id\":\"T007\",\"statement\":\"Function.Bijective (fun p : V n × ℝ => join p.1 p.2) ∧ (∀ (x : V n) (t : ℝ), base (join x t) = x ∧ height (join x t) = t) ∧ (∀ z : V (n+1), join (base z) (height z) = z) ∧ (∀ (x y : V n) (t u : ℝ), dist (join x t) (join y u) ^ 2 = dist x y ^ 2 + dist t u ^ 2)\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S007\"],\"explanation\":\"Fin.lastCases concatenation and coordinate restriction are inverse, and the finite sum of squared coordinate differences splits into its first n coordinates and last coordinate. This is the checked Euclidean product representation bridge; the domain pair type is used only as a set of coordinates, not with its native max metric.\"},{\"id\":\"T008\",\"statement\":\"(0 ≤ dimH E ∧ dimH E ≤ (n : ℝ≥0∞)) ∧ dimH E ≠ ⊤ ∧ Bornology.IsBounded E ∧ (∃ R : ℝ, 1 ≤ R ∧ ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ j : Fin n, |x j| ≤ R) ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E < ⊤) ∧ (n = 0 → E = Set.univ ∧ dimH E = 0) ∧ Function.Bijective (fun p : V n × ℝ => join p.1 p.2) ∧ (∀ (x : V n) (t : ℝ), base (join x t) = x ∧ height (join x t) = t) ∧ (∀ z : V (n+1), join (base z) (height z) = z) ∧ (∀ (x y : V n) (t u : ℝ), dist (join x t) (join y u) ^ 2 = dist x y ^ 2 + dist t u ^ 2)\",\"inputs\":[\"T005\",\"T006\",\"T007\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\",\"S006\",\"S007\"],\"explanation\":\"Assemble every N002 clause. The dimension bound by the finite natural n gives finiteness; the inverse and squared-distance identities express exactly the bijective Euclidean concatenation isometry without installing a different product metric.\"},{\"id\":\"T009\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q r ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < r → r ≤ 1 → 0 < ε → ∃ W : ℕ → Set (V n), scaleCover r E W ∧ cost q W < ENNReal.ofReal (ε/2)\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S008\"],\"explanation\":\"q>D forces q>0, and the native critical-exponent statement gives zero Hausdorff measure and zero content. Extract an admissible sequence of cost below ε/2 from the infimum.\"},{\"id\":\"T010\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q r ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < r → r ≤ 1 → 0 < ε → ∃ K : Set ℕ, ∃ U : K → Set (V n), E ⊆ (⋃ i, U i) ∧ (∀ i, (U i).Nonempty ∧ 0 < Metric.ediam (U i) ∧ Metric.ediam (U i) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal r) ∧ cost q U < ENNReal.ofReal ε\",\"inputs\":[\"T009\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S009\"],\"explanation\":\"N003: remove empty members using a subtype of ℕ. Replace zero-diameter singletons by small closed balls as in S009. In n≥1 opposite first-coordinate points certify the prescribed positive diameter. Bound extra cost by the geometric error budget. Subtypes of ℕ capture arbitrary countable index sets up to enumeration.\"},{\"id\":\"T011\",\"statement\":\"n = 0 → dimH (cylinder E) ≤ dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S010\"],\"explanation\":\"For the unique base point partition the interval into m pieces; joined pieces have diameters 1/m by the squared-distance identity. For a>1 the total cost tends to zero, giving the zero-ambient-dimensional upper bound.\"},{\"id\":\"T012\",\"statement\":\"1 ≤ n → ∀ q δ ε : ℝ, dimH E < ENNReal.ofReal q → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → ∃ W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1)), scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W ∧ cost (q+1) W < ENNReal.ofReal (2 * (Real.sqrt 2) ^ (q+1) * ε)\",\"inputs\":[\"T010\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S011\"],\"explanation\":\"Take r=min(1,δ/sqrt 2), and the N003 cover. For each member of diameter r_i, use ceil(1/r_i) subintervals and their joined products. Enumerate the countable sigma family with empty padding. The squared-distance identity bounds product diameters by sqrt(2)r_i; ceil(1/r_i)≤2/r_i yields the stated cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T013\",\"statement\":\"dimH (cylinder E) ≤ dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T011\",\"T012\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S012\"],\"explanation\":\"N004: let ε decrease to zero at fixed q>D and δ>0, then take the supremum over scales and the dimension bound at exponent q+1. Finiteness of D and order density allow q to decrease to D. Combine n=0 and n≥1.\"},{\"id\":\"T014\",\"statement\":\"MeasurableSet unitI ∧ (volume unitI : ℝ≥0∞) = 1\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S013\"],\"explanation\":\"Use Borel measurability of Icc and Real.volume_Icc.\"},{\"id\":\"T015\",\"statement\":\"(MeasurableSet unitI ∧ (volume unitI : ℝ≥0∞) = 1) ∧ (∀ (B : Set ℝ) (ρ η : ℝ), B.Nonempty → 0 ≤ ρ → Metric.ediam B ≤ ENNReal.ofReal ρ → 0 < η → Bornology.IsBounded B ∧ BddBelow B ∧ BddAbove B ∧ (∀ b ∈ B, sInf B ≤ b ∧ b ≤ sSup B) ∧ sSup B - sInf B ≤ ρ ∧ (let J := Set.Ioo (sInf B - η/2) (sSup B + η/2); B ⊆ J ∧ MeasurableSet J ∧ 0 < sSup B - sInf B + η ∧ sSup B - sInf B + η ≤ ρ + η ∧ (volume J : ℝ≥0∞) = ENNReal.ofReal (sSup B - sInf B + η) ∧ (volume (J ∩ unitI) : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (ρ + η) ∧ MeasurableSet (J ∩ unitI)))\",\"inputs\":[\"T014\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S014\"],\"explanation\":\"N005: finite ediam and nonemptiness imply boundedness. Real infimum/supremum inequalities give sup-inf≤ρ. The displayed open interval encloses B and has length at most ρ+η; apply volume_Ioo and measure monotonicity. No measurability of B is used.\"},{\"id\":\"T016\",\"statement\":\"LipschitzWith 1 (base (n := n)) ∧ LipschitzWith 1 (height (n := n)) ∧ (∀ (δ : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < δ → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → ∀ i, Metric.ediam (W i) = ENNReal.ofReal (Metric.diam (W i)) ∧ 0 ≤ Metric.diam (W i) ∧ Metric.diam (W i) ≤ δ ∧ Metric.ediam (base '' W i) ≤ Metric.ediam (W i) ∧ Metric.ediam (height '' W i) ≤ Metric.ediam (W i))\",\"inputs\":[\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S015\"],\"explanation\":\"The coordinate splitting of squared Euclidean distances gives 1-Lipschitz projections. Their image diameter bounds follow. At finite scale every ediam is finite, so Metric.diam and ENNReal.ofReal are exact inverse descriptions; this is the explicit real/extended-diameter conversion used in the error budget.\"},{\"id\":\"T017\",\"statement\":\"∀ (s δ ε : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < s → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → ∃ J : ℕ → Set ℝ, (∀ i, MeasurableSet (J i) ∧ height '' W i ⊆ J i ∧ (volume (J i ∩ unitI) : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (Metric.diam (W i) + padding ε s W i)) ∧ (∀ t ∈ unitI, content s δ E ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i) (fun _ : ℝ => (Metric.ediam (W i)) ^ s) t)\",\"inputs\":[\"T015\",\"T016\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S016\"],\"explanation\":\"Use η_i=ε2^(-i-1)/(1+ρ_i^s)>0, explicit enclosures for nonempty W_i and J_i=∅ otherwise. For every t∈I, choose projected sets when t∈J_i and empty sets otherwise. This is a scale-δ cover of E and its content cost is bounded by the indicator series. Arbitrary covering sets and their projections need not be measurable.\"},{\"id\":\"T018\",\"statement\":\"∀ (c : ℝ≥0∞) (w : ℕ → ℝ≥0∞) (J : ℕ → Set ℝ), (∀ i, MeasurableSet (J i)) → (∀ t ∈ unitI, c ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i) (fun _ : ℝ => w i) t) → ((∀ i, Measurable (Set.indicator (J i ∩ unitI) (fun _ : ℝ => w i))) ∧ (∀ t : ℝ, Set.indicator unitI (fun _ : ℝ => c) t ≤ ∑' i, Set.indicator (J i ∩ unitI) (fun _ : ℝ => w i) t) ∧ c ≤ ∑' i, w i * volume (J i ∩ unitI))\",\"inputs\":[\"T014\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S017\",\"S018\"],\"explanation\":\"Prove measurability of the Borel indicators and the pointwise inequality on all ℝ by cases t∈I. Integrate using lintegral_mono, lintegral_tsum and lintegral_indicator_const, and volume(I)=1. This nonnegative Tonelli statement allows infinite c and infinite series and requires no finite integrability.\"},{\"id\":\"T019\",\"statement\":\"∀ (s δ ε : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set (V (n+1))), 0 < s → 0 < δ → 0 < ε → scaleCover δ (cylinder E) W → content s δ E ≤ cost (s+1) W + ENNReal.ofReal ε\",\"inputs\":[\"T017\",\"T018\",\"T016\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S018\"],\"explanation\":\"Insert the enclosures into T018. With finite ρ_i convert the real algebra η_iρ_i^s≤ε2^(-i-1) back to ENNReal, and use ρ_i^sρ_i=ρ_i^(s+1). Sum using nonnegative tsum comparisons and the geometric sum, valid also for infinite total cost.\"},{\"id\":\"T020\",\"statement\":\"∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → 0 < δ → content s δ E ≤ content (s+1) δ (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T019\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S019\"],\"explanation\":\"N006: for each admissible W remove the arbitrary positive ε using order density (infinite cost is immediate). Infimize over W. Retain exactly the same scale on both sides.\"},{\"id\":\"T021\",\"statement\":\"0 < dimH E → ∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → ENNReal.ofReal s < dimH E → haus (s+1) (cylinder E) = ⊤ ∧ ENNReal.ofReal (s+1) ≤ dimH (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T020\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S020\"],\"explanation\":\"For 0<s<D criticality gives haus s E=∞. Take suprema in the same-scale slicing inequality, then apply criticality at s+1.\"},{\"id\":\"T022\",\"statement\":\"0 < dimH E → dimH E + 1 ≤ dimH (cylinder E)\",\"inputs\":[\"T021\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S021\"],\"explanation\":\"N007: finite positive D is the supremum of the positive real s<D. Apply order density to the inequalities at s+1.\"},{\"id\":\"T023\",\"statement\":\"(∀ a : ℝ, 1 < a → haus a unitI = 0) ∧ dimH unitI ≤ 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\"],\"explanation\":\"Divide [0,1] into m equal intervals. Their a-cost is m^(1-a), tending to zero, and diameters tend to zero. Apply criticality and order density.\"},{\"id\":\"T024\",\"statement\":\"∀ (δ : ℝ) (W : ℕ → Set ℝ), 0 < δ → scaleCover δ unitI W → (1 : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ cost 1 W\",\"inputs\":[\"T015\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S023\"],\"explanation\":\"Enclose each nonempty member by an open interval with error ε2^(-i-1), use volume(I)=1 and countable subadditivity, sum the geometric errors and remove ε. This proves total diameter at least one without assuming the cover measurable.\"},{\"id\":\"T025\",\"statement\":\"(∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → 0 < δ → ENNReal.ofReal (δ ^ (s-1)) ≤ content s δ unitI) ∧ (∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → haus s unitI = ⊤) ∧ (1 : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ dimH unitI\",\"inputs\":[\"T024\",\"T004\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S024\"],\"explanation\":\"At finite scale compare ρ_i^s≥δ^(s-1)ρ_i, including ρ_i=0 separately. Sum and infimize. The negative-power limit gives infinite Hausdorff measure and criticality gives dimension at least one.\"},{\"id\":\"T026\",\"statement\":\"(∀ a : ℝ, 1 < a → haus a unitI = 0) ∧ (∀ s δ : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → 0 < δ → ENNReal.ofReal (δ ^ (s-1)) ≤ content s δ unitI) ∧ (∀ s : ℝ, 0 < s → s < 1 → haus s unitI = ⊤) ∧ dimH unitI = 1\",\"inputs\":[\"T023\",\"T025\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\",\"S023\",\"S024\"],\"explanation\":\"N008: assemble the interval content and measure assertions and the equality of upper and lower dimension bounds.\"},{\"id\":\"T027\",\"statement\":\"dimH (cylinder E) = dimH E + 1\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T013\",\"T022\",\"T026\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S025\"],\"explanation\":\"If D>0 apply N007. If D=0 choose x₀∈E, use the squared-distance identity to show t↦join x₀ t is an isometry on I, and its image lies in cylinder E. Isometry invariance, monotonicity and N008 yield dimension at least one. Combine with N004; T008 identifies exactly the stipulated Euclidean target, including n=0.\"}]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/source_outputs\",\"value\":{\"N001\":\"T004\",\"N002\":\"T008\",\"N003\":\"T010\",\"N004\":\"T013\",\"N005\":\"T015\",\"N006\":\"T020\",\"N007\":\"T022\",\"N008\":\"T026\",\"P\":\"T027\"}}]}}","status":"completed"}}

